  "narrative": "All newly delivered estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For the direction -1 claims assessed here, a positive estimate means the exposed group has stronger dependency, i.e. a more negative dependency score, than the comparator group. H116 and H117 directly stratify PTEN-loss STAT3-dependency association by TP53-loss status. H116 gives a positive estimate of +0.1903, 95% interval +0.0851 to +0.2955, in TP53-loss cells; H117 gives +0.2870, interval +0.2273 to +0.3467, in TP53-intact cells. Both exclude zero and are large relative to the STAT3 dependency distribution (SD approximately 0.246). I therefore accept both as within-dataset positive associations. The TP53-loss effect is numerically smaller, which is consistent with the negative point estimate of the H95 interaction; however, that interaction interval includes zero, so attenuation remains suggestive rather than established. I requested independent validation of H116 because it is the most informative unresolved TP53-context estimate and will clarify whether the PTEN/STAT3 association is robust in TP53-loss cells or whether it should be narrowed to TP53-intact contexts. H118 and H119 further show that the PTEN-loss STAT3 association persists in PIK3CA-wild-type cells (+0.2670) and KRAS-mutant cells (+0.2486), with intervals excluding zero. These results support the general PTEN-loss/STAT3 association across major RAS and PI3K genotypic contexts, rather than restricting it to PIK3CA-wild-type or KRAS-wild-type cells. H104 tests PIK3CA mutation association with STAT3 dependency among PTEN-intact cells and gives a near-zero estimate (-0.0066; interval -0.0289 to +0.0157). I reject the positive-association claim and close it. This strengthens the specificity of PTEN loss as the relevant predictor, rather than a broad PI3K-pathway mutation effect. H120 and H121 test whether PTEN loss predicts MDM2 and NFE2L2 dependency. Both are null or very small: MDM2 -0.0148 (interval -0.0433 to +0.0137), NFE2L2 -0.0479 (interval -0.0964 to +0.0006). The NFE2L2 estimate is in the opposite direction and only borderline excludes stronger dependency; it is not scientifically meaningful as a broad PTEN effect and should not be interpreted as a robust weaker-dependency finding. I reject the registered positive PTEN-loss stronger-dependency claims and close them. H122 through H126 test cross-specificity of the leading accepted predictors: TP53-wild-type versus SMARCA2/WRN, SMARCA4-loss versus USP7, MSI-high versus SMARCA2, and KEAP1-loss versus USP7. All estimates are near zero with intervals spanning zero. I reject the positive association claims and close these as outcome-specificity checks. Collectively, the evidence supports a focused set of within-dataset associations: TP53-wild-type with USP7, SMARCA4-loss with SMARCA2, MSI-high with WRN, KEAP1-loss with weaker NFE2L2, and PTEN-loss with stronger STAT3. The new results show PTEN-loss/STAT3 is positive in both TP53 strata, though possibly attenuated in TP53-loss cells, and is not explained by PIK3CA mutation or a broader dependency-state association with the other leading outcomes. These are associations in the supplied dataset, not causal claims, drug-response claims, or externally generalizable biomarker statements.",
  "research_notes": "Current supported within-dataset associations: TP53-WT stronger USP7; SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2; MSI-high stronger WRN; KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2; PTEN-loss stronger STAT3. Direction convention: for direction -1 main effects, positive signed estimate means exposed has stronger (more negative) dependency; for direction +1, positive signed estimate means weaker (less negative) dependency. PTEN/STAT3 robustness: overall accepted and validated H94; positive in high-quality screens, KRAS-WT, non-KY library, batch_a, adherent culture, adenocarcinoma, PIK3CA-WT H118, KRAS-mutant H119, TP53-intact H117, and TP53-loss H116. The direct strata suggest attenuation in TP53-loss cells (+0.1903 with 54 exposed) relative to TP53-intact cells (+0.2870 with 212 exposed), matching the negative but imprecise H95 interaction point estimate. H116 independent validation requested to determine whether the TP53-loss stratum remains positive at stricter validation-adjusted precision. If validated, PTEN/STAT3 remains general across TP53 contexts; if not, keep overall PTEN/STAT3 accepted but mark TP53-loss stratum unresolved/attenuated. A reversed TP53-interaction attenuation proposal was registered against H95, reflecting the same data with opposite sign; its current evidence is suggestive, not conclusive. Specificity: PTEN loss is near null or very small against KRAS, USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, MDM2, and NFE2L2; PIK3CA mutation is null for STAT3 among PTEN-intact cells. Cross-specificity of leading accepted predictors is null for TP53-WT/SMARCA2, TP53-WT/WRN, SMARCA4-loss/USP7, MSI-high/SMARCA2, and KEAP1-loss/USP7, supporting narrow outcome-specific associations rather than broad dependency-state effects. Remaining registered probes: CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 among TP53-WT and research-marker-D/STAT3. Remaining broad STAT3 predictor probes H105-H112 should be prioritized selectively, not tested indiscriminately, to limit multiplicity. Avoid causal, drug-response, and external generalizability language; emphasize dataset association, effect size relative to outcome SD, and uncertainty.",
